            <ArticleTitle>The barrier-belief approach in the counseling of physical activity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>129-36</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pec.2014.10.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0738-3991(14)00410-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To understand inactivity and relapse from PA, and to develop theory-based behavior change strategies to stimulate and support maintenance of PA.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We conducted a literature search to explore barriers to PA. Social cognitive theories and empirical evidence were evaluated and guided the process developing a theoretical framework and counseling strategies.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A theoretical framework is presented to understand why people do not engage in PA and often relapse once they started PA. A distinction is made between three related types of BBs. In PA counseling these three beliefs are addressed using four different BB behavior change strategies.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">BB counseling aims to develop an individual pattern of PA for the long term that is adapted to the (often limited) motivation of the client, thereby preventing the occurrence of BBs. The client will learn to cope with factors that may inhibit PA in the future.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PRACTICE IMPLICATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The BBs approach composes a way of counseling around the central construct of barrier-beliefs to stimulate engagement in PA independently, in the long term.</AbstractText>
